Subject: VMS: rename() that changes only the case lost the file's name

With 'fileignorecase' (always on VMS), vim_rename() renames a file whose
new name differs only in case through a temporary name: "from" with its
tail replaced by a number. VMS rename() takes a missing file type from the
old name, so "C.VIM" became "123.VIM" while Vim then looked for "123": the
second step and the move back both failed, rename() returned -1 and the
file was left as 123.VIM (a directory as 123.DIR).

Keep the old name's type in the temporary name on VMS, or give an explicit
null type ("123.").

--- a/src/fileio.c
+++ b/src/fileio.c
@@ -3936,7 +3936,19 @@
 	STRCPY(tempname, from);
 	for (n = 123; n < 99999; ++n)
 	{
+#ifdef VMS
+	    // rename() takes a missing file type from the old name: without
+	    // one "C.VIM" became "123.VIM" while Vim looked for "123", and then
+	    // neither step could find it.  Keep the type (".DIR" for a
+	    // directory), or give an explicit null one.
+	    char_u  *tail = gettail((char_u *)tempname);
+	    char_u  *ext = vim_strrchr(gettail(from), '.');
+
+	    vim_snprintf((char *)tail, sizeof(tempname) - (tail - (char_u *)tempname),
+				   "%d%s", n, ext == NULL ? "." : (char *)ext);
+#else
 	    sprintf((char *)gettail((char_u *)tempname), "%d", n);
+#endif
 	    if (mch_stat(tempname, &st) < 0)
 	    {
 		if (mch_rename((char *)from, tempname) == 0)
